{
  "id": "COMPOUND:27545 - PROTEIN:6218",
  "verbalization": "Zeylanol is shown to have multiple targets that ultimately lead to the Induced myeloid leukemia cell differentiation protein Mcl-1. The evidence suggests that Zeylanol may inhibit the activity of ADP-ribosylation factor-like protein 3, which in turn is targeted by (-)-Fragransin A2 and Isorhamnetin 3-robinobioside. These compounds are implicated in the regulation of Mcl-1, a key protein involved in the survival of myeloid leukemia cells. The integration of these pathways indicates that Zeylanol may act through a series of molecular interactions that ultimately target Mcl-1, potentially inducing cell differentiation in leukemia cells. The cumulative evidence supports a mechanistic link between Zeylanol and Mcl-1, though the exact mode of action and the role of the intermediate targets require further investigation to confirm the direct involvement of Mcl-1 in the observed effects.",
